  "step_1": "We assess whether the decision reason—considering skewness—can be applied strictly. Skewness in this context is defined as a greater portion of probability being assigned to favorable outcomes compared to unfavorable ones. In Lottery A, the outcomes are 2500, 2400, and 0 Euros with probabilities 33%, 66%, and 1% respectively. The favorable outcomes (2500 and 2400) together have 99% of the probability, with 66% on 2400 and 33% on 2500. The unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) has only 1%. Thus, the majority of the probability mass is on favorable outcomes, especially concentrated on 2400 and 2500. This indicates a left-skew (since higher values are more probable). In Lottery B, the outcome is 2400 Euros with 100% probability—this is a single point mass, not spread across outcomes. A single point mass has no skew in the traditional sense; it is symmetric or degenerate. Since there is no variation, it does not exhibit left-skewness. Therefore, the skewness of Lottery A is clearly greater than that of Lottery B. The information is derivable and applicable.",
  "step_2": "The decision reason prefers the lottery that is more left-skewed. Lottery A has a higher concentration of probability on favorable outcomes (2500 and 2400) with only 1% on 0 Euros, indicating a left-skewed distribution. Lottery B is a degenerate distribution with no skew. Therefore, the reason prefers Lottery A.",
  "final_answer": "A"
}

{
